"Shows how a specific business model creates, delivers and captures value through nine interdependent building blocks.\n\nUse when: Explain the business model behind one offer. Compare alternative ways of serving a customer segment.\n\nAvoid when: A detailed operating procedure or validated financial forecast is required.\n\nVisual grammar: Nine labeled blocks separate customers, value, channels, relationships, revenue, resources, activities, partners and costs. Notes describe hypotheses within their appropriate blocks. Consistent markers distinguish assumptions from verified evidence.\n\nBuild: Choose one model and identify its customer segments. Describe the offer and the delivery arrangements that make it possible. Connect revenue logic and cost drivers, then flag assumptions requiring tests.\n\nCheck: Every value proposition serves an identified customer segment. Activities, resources and partners can support the proposed delivery. Revenue and costs describe the same model boundary.\n\nPitfalls: Combining several incompatible businesses into one crowded canvas. Treating a completed canvas as evidence that demand exists.\n\nExample: A repair subscription pairs homeowners with predictable maintenance, using local technicians, recurring fees and clear delivery costs.\n\nAlternatives: Value Proposition Canvas, Lean Canvas."
